\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\title{Controlled reset of search state}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{harness}
\tag{state}
\tag{evaluation}

\p{A lifecycle comparison must distinguish the state that proposes a candidate
from the state that stores a candidate, its workspace, and its evaluation
history. Let a search state at round #{t}, under fixed verifier/reducer law
#{\mathcal V}, be}
##{
  Z_t=(s_t,h_t,w_t,b_t,q_t,\rho_t;\mathcal V),
}
\p{where #{s_t} is the session context visible to the proposer, #{h_t} is the
candidate-history record, #{w_t} is the workspace and filesystem snapshot,
#{b_t} is the retained best candidate together with its score, artifact digest,
and required verifier status, #{q_t} is mutable reducer state, and #{\rho_t}
is the random-source state. The law/configuration #{\mathcal V} is immutable;
the reset recomputes #{q_0} rather than carrying mutable reducer pointers.
Candidate visibility and reducer behavior are part of the record: a
candidate that is scored but not shown to a later proposer is not the same state
as a candidate that remains in its context.}

\p{A reset at boundary #{r} is a typed transition}
##{
  \mathsf{Reset}_r(Z_r)=
  (s_0,h_0,w_0,b_r,q_0,\rho'_r;\mathcal V),
}
\p{that retains the declared best candidate #{b_r} and its content-addressed
artifact, while clearing the named components. Here #{h_0} excludes discarded
history and retained-best metadata, and #{w_0} is a clean workspace containing
only the verified materialization of #{b_r}; #{h_0} contains no duplicate of
#{b_r}. For a training-script task, runtime checkpoints and other generated
outputs remain in #{w_t} and are cleared unless the protocol explicitly declares
them part of #{b_r}. The retained artifact and its materialization are counted
explicitly as retained storage. The transition records the
parent and child hashes, the discarded bytes and tokens, the reducer and
visibility rules, and the random-source change. It does not erase the rejected
proposals or their execution and evaluation costs from the lifecycle vector in
\ref{ftip-005H}. A reset is therefore an intervention on state lifecycle, not
evidence that the solver or proposer learned a new general algorithm.}

\p{The source observation in \citet{Abstract; Sections 3--5 and Tables 1--3}{tan2026forgetting}
uses the Pi agent with DeepSeek V4.1 Flash on four bounded tasks spanning
heuristic programming, kernel optimization, and model training, with the same
budget in a single-agent run, four parallel agents, and a four-agent team that
forgets three times; it reports three runs per task and configuration. Its
session-and-history intervention carries forward the best candidate. That
observation motivates, but does not test, the following paired FTIP decomposition
arms:}
\ol{
  \li{continue all declared state;}
  \li{clear session context only (new FTIP arm);}
  \li{clear candidate history only (new FTIP arm); and}
  \li{clear session, candidate history, and workspace state while retaining the
  best candidate, score, status, and artifact digest (new FTIP arm).}
}

\p{For a fixed non-random endowment #{E_0}, verifier law/configuration
#{\mathcal V}, proposal budget #{B}, and task law #{Q}, the primary finite
estimand is the paired reset contrast under paired randomization law
#{\mathcal R}:}
##{
  \Delta_r=\mathbb E[U\mid \mathsf{Reset}_r,E_0,\mathcal V,B,Q,\mathcal R]
  -\mathbb E[U\mid \mathsf{Continue},E_0,\mathcal V,B,Q,\mathcal R].
}
\p{The expectation is indexed by #{\mathcal R}, which assigns paired runs and
their random-source draws; it is not defined by the informal instruction to
reuse a seed. A run reports the best-candidate
utility, proposal and verifier outcomes, realized token/tool/storage cost, and
the complete state and artifact hashes. A noisy or deceptive verifier is a
future stress arm for this estimand; it is not an assumption that the reset is
beneficial.}

\p{The source does not identify whether its gain comes from session length,
candidate visibility, filesystem persistence, proposal diversity, or an
interaction among them. The four-arm decomposition is an FTIP protocol proposal.
Without the isolated arms, the source supports a bounded reset observation but
not a theorem that memory is harmful, a monotone law for forgetting, or a
general self-improvement claim.}
